AB  - The aerosol and cloud have been shown to influence the gross primary productivity (GPP) in terrestrial ecosystems. The common methods are physical and vegetation process models, which are restricted only to the surrounding area of study. To overcome these geographical and scalar limitations, we establish several machine learning models to assess the effect of aerosol optical depth (AOD) and cloud optical depth (COD) on GPP. Results showed that LightGBM performed better than other models, and contribution uncertainty (CU) is less than 15 % for 81.7 % AOD and 80.5 % COD. Both AOD and COD demonstrated an inhibitory effect on GPP (Delta GPPAOD: 3.46 g C/m2, Delta GPPCOD : 14.96 g C/m2), largely dispersed in the Congo Basin, North America, and Southeast Asia. Analysis of extensive data revealed two critical thresholds for Delta GPP response to AOD/COD gradients. The first threshold marks the transition from low suppression to diffuse fertilization enhancement, other indicates the return to suppression under heavy loading. Comparing the threshold for different plant types revealed that Forests with high canopy structures and farmland types with high leaf area index have a stronger positive regulatory capacity for AOD, while shrubs are more sensitive. For most vegetation, the inhibitory effect of COD on GPP is more significant and stronger than that of AOD. The results of this study help to understand the impact mechanisms of AOD and COD on the terrestrial carbon cycle.
