AB  - Effective management of Indoor Air Quality (IAQ), critical to public health, is hindered by the challenge of characterizing complex and dynamic pollutant dispersion pathways. However, there's a lack of simple and efficient tools to characterize and analyze indoor pollutant dispersion pathways. To address this gap, this study develops an explainable hybrid spatiotemporal prediction model framework, X-GAT-GRU, which integrates spatio-temporal networks to learn indoor dispersion characteristics using a graph attention mechanism. To assess the analytical and interpretive capabilities of the model framework, a case evaluation was performed in an indoor environment characterized by explicit dispersion pathways, with the model driven by low-cost sensor (LCS) data. The LCS network collected PM2.5 concentrations, temperature and humidity at a one-second resolution, while concurrently recording the window/door status. The results show that the X-GAT-GRU model can predict indoor PM2.5 concentrations in the case study with an R2=0.80. The model also dynamically characterizes pollutant dispersion pathways between rooms, revealing how pollutants originating from emission events travel along dispersion pathways and continuously altered by the building's ventilation status, validating the correlation between physical concentration gradients and the model's attention weights. This model framework leverages data-driven approaches to analyze the dynamic dispersion pathways of pollutants within buildings. This facilitates a shift in IAQ management from passive response to proactive intervention, for instance, by strategically adding air purifiers and ventilation at critical dispersion nodes. Furthermore, this framework offers a novel approach for analyzing nodal relationships in models that can be simplified into topological networks.
