AB  - Airborne pathogenic microbial aerosols (PMA) carrying pathogenic microorganisms are a significant class of biopollutants and a key pathway for respiratory disease transmission, posing a serious threat to public health. Low-temperature plasma (LTP) possesses broad-spectrum antibacterial activity and is independent of chemical reagents, thus it is considered a promising disinfection method. However, the disinfection effect of LTP is influenced by various operating parameters, and the underlying nonlinear mechanisms remain unclear. This study combines machine learning methods with LTP disinfection experiments to systematically optimize its treatment performance. We constructed a multi-model stacking framework based on eight machine learning algorithms, adjusted hyperparameters by reserving a validation set, and evaluated model performance on an independent test set. Results show that the CatBoost model provides the most accurate prediction of disinfection efficiency. Feature importance analysis based on the SHAP method indicates that plasma power, treatment time, discharge voltage, and gas flow rate are the main variables affecting disinfection effectiveness. The proposed machine learning optimization method not only improves prediction performance but also provides an effective approach for parameter optimization of the LTP disinfection system. These results demonstrate that data-driven models have significant potential in guiding the development of plasma technology and providing new strategies for controlling airborne microbial pollution. This research contributes to advancements in intelligent environmental disinfection systems and supports the development of cleaner, more efficient air quality management technologies.
