AB  - This study examines a decade (2015-2024) of ground-based aerosol observations from the CIMEL sunphotometer at the Chiang Mai AERONET station, located in an urbanizing city in northern Thailand. The aerosol optical depth at 500 nm (AOD500) exhibits distinct seasonal variation, peaking during the pre-monsoon season due to biomass burning and declining in the post-monsoon. The annual average AOD500 is 0.52 +/- 0.47, with an increasing trend of +0.0098 per year, reflecting rising regional aerosol emissions. Angstro<spacing diaeresis>m exponent values (AE440-870) show a seasonal dominance of fine mode aerosols during winter and pre-monsoon, linked to urban and biomass burning sources, and a shift to coarse mode aerosols during the monsoon and post-monsoon, associated with natural processes. The annual average AE440-870 is 1.48 +/- 0.28. Aerosol classification based on the AOD and AE method, as well as the extinction Angstro<spacing diaeresis>m exponent (EAE) and absorption Angstro<spacing diaeresis>m exponent (AAE) method, indicates that biomass burning is the dominant source during the pre-monsoon season, whereas winter is primarily influenced by urban/industrial aerosols under stagnant meteorological conditions. Seasonal variations in single scattering albedo (SSA), asymmetry factor (g), refractive index (m), and volume size distribution (VSD) further reflect shifts in aerosol types and sources. These characteristics demonstrate the influence of urban emissions, biomass burning, regional transport, and seasonal meteorology on aerosol composition. The annual average aerosol radiative forcing (ARF) within the atmosphere is +68.95 +/- 46.33 W/m2, with a corresponding radiative forcing efficiency (ARFE) of 161.23 +/- 59.50 W/m2/AOD, indicating a significant atmospheric warming effect. These findings enhance understanding of aerosol-climate interactions in tropical urbanizing environments and underscore the impacts of anthropogenic activities on air quality and radiative forcing. (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
