TI  - Nonlinear impact of block spatial form on carbon pollution synergies in mining cities-An integrated analysis based on GWRF and SHAP
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
LA  - English
KW  - Block spatial form
KW  - Carbon emissions
KW  - PM2.5
KW  - Coupling coordination degree
KW  - Geographically weighted random forest
KW  - Mining city
KW  - AIR-QUALITY
KW  - URBAN FORM
AB  - Optimizing the spatial layout of mining cities is crucial for achieving sustainable development and pollution reduction and carbon emission reduction goals. However, the nonlinear effects of spatial form on the synergistic relationship between carbon emission intensity (CEI) and PM2.5 at the block scale, and the spatial heterogeneity of these effects remain unresolved in current research. This study focuses on seven representative mining cities in China and develops a coupling coordination degree (CCD) model to quantify the synergistic effect of carbon pollution. The study innovatively combines the geographically weighted random forest (GWRF) model with the SHAP explanation framework to systematically analyze the nonlinear influence mechanisms and spatial variation patterns of block spatial form indicators. The results show that: (1) The GWRF model improves R-2 by approximately 38.59% compared to the global model. The mean building height (MH) is identified as the key driver, with the importance ranking of the factors as follows: MH > road network density (RND) > building density (BD) > standard deviation height (SDH) > green space rate (GSR) > mean building surface area (MBS) > open space rate (OSR); (2) The synergistic effect of CEI and PM2.5 is more pronounced in mining cities in southern regions. Three distinct types of high-value CCD areas are identified: multi-center distribution, single-core aggregation, and axial extension; (3) MH is significantly positively correlated with CCD across all cities, with the greatest influence occurring in the 10-12 m range. RND is positively correlated with CCD in the 0-0.002 km/km(2) range, while BD is in the 0-12% range. GSR shows a negative correlation with CCD, with an average threshold of approximately 15%. (4) Natural and socioeconomic contexts shape the CEI-PM2.5 synergy. In northern cities, BD dominates the CEI-PM2.5 synergy; in southern cities, MH dominates. The results of this localized analysis and the identification of nonlinear effects provide valuable insights for the development of more targeted policies aimed at pollution and carbon reduction.
