AB  - Air pollution remains a pressing environmental challenge with direct consequences for vegetation health. Plants experience physiological stress under polluted conditions, which can disrupt their growth, development, and resilience. This study investigates the relationship between atmospheric aerosols and vegetation health in the southwestern region of West Bengal by integrating multi-source remote sensing data. MODIS aerosol records from 2000 to 2023 were analyzed to assess spatial and temporal variations in AOD. Vegetation condition was evaluated using Landsat-5 and Landsat-8 imagery through indices such as NDVI, EVI, SAVI, NDMI, NDWI, TCI, VCI, VHI, and LST. In addition, Sentinel-5P data were employed to examine the influence of major pollutants-including sulfur dioxide, nitrogen dioxide, carbon monoxide, ozone, methane, formaldehyde, and the aerosol index-using the GEE platform. Results reveal pronounced seasonal variability: AOD values peaked at 1.230 in Durgapur during July (monsoon) and dropped below 0.3 in Purulia during March (pre-monsoon). Monsoon months consistently exhibited higher aerosol loads (>0.9), while pre-monsoon conditions reflected comparatively lower concentrations. Long-term analysis indicates a rising trend in aerosol levels across the study area. Multicorrelation analysis further highlights significant associations between AOD fluctuations and vegetation health indicators, confirming that elevated aerosol concentrations correspond to measurable declines in plant vitality. The findings underscore the effectiveness of integrating time-series satellite observations with advanced cloud-computing platforms for environmental monitoring, and they provide an (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar
