AB  - Urbanization-driven temperature rise has emerged as a pressing environmental challenge. However, the multifaceted impacts of vertical urban forms-particularly its interaction effects with urban climate-remain underexplored. This study investigates both the individual and interaction effects of vertical urban form variables-Vertical (building height), Variance (building height variability), Volume, and Vacant (spatial openness)- on urban temperatures in Seoul, South Korea. An Artificial Neural Network (ANN) model, interpreted using Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), was employed to identify the non-linear effects among urban forms. In summer, spatial openness was identified as the most influential factor. Interaction analysis revealed that areas with below-average openness (<41.3 %) were highly sensitive to reductions in building height, experiencing sharp temperature increases, especially when the building height fell below approximately 10 m. Densely built, low-rise areas with uniform building heights and low openness were found to be most vulnerable to overheating. In winter, building volume emerged as the dominant vertical variable affecting temperature, with increased PM2.5 concentrations also contributing to localized warming. The SHAP analysis elucidated novel non-linear interactions effects, demonstrating that increasing building height to enhance openness-despite increasing volume-can yield a net thermal benefit in summer due to the dominant cooling effect of greater building height. These findings underscore the necessity of integrating nuanced, interaction-aware vertical urban design strategies into urban planning to effectively regulate local thermal environments and foster climate-resilient cities.
