AB  - Aerosols significantly impact the regional radiation budget and global climate change. The aerosol extinction coefficient, which quantifies the absorption and scattering of light by aerosol particles in the atmosphere, is essential for environment monitoring and atmospheric optics research. While satellite retrievals, such as the Cloud-Aerosol Lidar and Infrared Pathfinder Satellite Observation (CALIPSO) and the Stratospheric Aerosol and Gas Experiment (SAGE), provide high-precision measurements of aerosol extinction coefficients, their spatial coverage is inherently limited. Conversely, the MERRA-2 reanalysis data offers global 3-D aerosol mass mixing ratios but suffers from significant uncertainties. Statistics indicate that the correlation coefficient is only about 0.42 (0.36) between aerosol extinction coefficients calculated directly from MERRA-2 and those retrieved by CALIPSO (SAGE). To address this issue, this study employed a machine learning approach to integrate MERRA-2 data with CALIPSO and SAGE retrievals, producing an optimal full-coverage 3-D aerosol extinction coefficient dataset. The optimized extinction coefficients achieve significantly improved correlation coefficients of 0.70 with CALIPSO and 0.75 with SAGE retrievals. Importantly, the optimized results can reflect the extinction characteristics of faint aerosols, which are missed by CALIPSO L2 layer product. This study provides valuable 3-D data for further understanding global distribution of aerosols, aerosol-climate interactions, and atmospheric processes.
