AB  - Urban form significantly influences environmental challenges, such as fine particulate matter (PM2.5) concentration and the Urban Heat Island (UHI) effect. However, few studies have explored the synergistic effects of urban form's spatial configuration on PM2.5 concentration and UHI prediction, with most overlooking spatial autocorrelation in their analyses. This study calculates four built-up landscape pattern metrics, alongside built-up volume, built-up surface, and 2D and 3D greenery, to investigate their effects at various scales on PM2.5 concentration and UHI intensity in Vilnius, Lithuania. Using an explanatory machine learning method, we identified non-linear relationships between contributing factors and their impact on PM2.5 concentration and UHI, while accounting for spatial autocorrelation. Our findings reveal the consistent prominence of the largest patch index (LPI), percentage of landscape (PLAND), and built-up volume in prediction models, although their relative importance varies across scales. LPI is strongly associated with UHI, while built-up volume and PLAND are more influential in predicting PM2.5 at finer resolutions. Two-way interactions among leading variables indicate that high values of LPI or PLAND, combined with high built-up volume, are associated with increased PM2.5 concentration and UHI levels. Furthermore, areas with high tree cover height (TCH) and high built-up volume in urban canyons restrict airflow, exacerbating pollutant accumulation and increasing PM2.5 concentration.
