AB  - Fine particulate matter (PM2.5) oxidative potential (OPv) is a crucial health metric, varying significantly by emission source. While concentration-response functions (CRFs) exist linking PM2.5 mass to OPv (OPvPM2.5), the lack of source-resolved OPv (OPvsource) CRFs limits accurate risk assessment globally. We developed global OPvsource CRFs by integrating machine learning, statistical techniques, and comprehensive datasets of PM2.5 concentrations, source apportionment (50 countries), and measured OPv (>10,000 measurements in 29 countries). Our analysis revealed distinct intrinsic OP per unit mass (OPm) for major sectors, ranking: energy (ENE) > transportation (TRO) > industry (IND) > agriculture and residential combustion (AGS + RCO). Applying these CRFs to 2017 PM2.5 source data across 203 countries, we estimated the global average OPvsource at 0.78 nmol min(-1) m(-3) (95 % CI: [0.39, 1.2]). ENE (33 %) and AGS + RCO (31 %) were predominant contributors globally, with substantial national variations. Poisson regression demonstrated that OPvsource is a significantly stronger predictor of PM2.5-attributable mortality than PM2.5 concentration or OPvPM2.5. These findings establish source-resolved OPv as a critical, more accurate metric for assessing mortality risks, supporting targeted air quality policies and interventions to mitigate PM2.5 health burden.
