AB  - Climate change and air pollution are two interconnected pressing global challenges that significantly affect urban sustainability. Although their origins often overlap, the potential to address these issues through synergistic measures remains underexplored. This study investigates how digital transformation of companies influences the synergy between climate change mitigation and air pollution reduction. Digital transformation is measured by the proportion of digital intangibles in a company, which is derived from financial reporting details. Methodologically, the study employs a machine learning model with XGBoost (eXtreme Gradient Boosting) integrated with SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to analyze the complex relationships of the variables, with hyperparameter optimization using the Optuna framework. The results indicate that digital transformation of companies significantly reduces the synergy between PM2.5 and COQ emissions. Notably, internal innovation within companies proves more impactful than external factors in mitigating synergistic emissions. Digital transformation in the secondary sector plays a substantial role in emission reduction, whereas the information sector further strengthens these environmental benefits. This research highlights the critical role of digital transformation in promoting urban sustainability and provides policy recommendations for integrating digital strategies into environmental governance frameworks.
