AB  - Air pollutant concentrations exhibit complex spatiotemproal relationships which may dynamically change over time. This imposes a significant challenge to air quality modeling. Deep learning methods have shown great potential in air quality modeling for prediction and management. However, they often struggle with effectively capturing these dynamic spatiotemporal relationships and suffer from limited interpretability. We propose an interpretable spatiotemporal air pollution modeling method, which uses the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and a composite attention mechanism to extract temporal and spatial information, respectively. Specifically, the composite attention mechanism incorporates two modules to separately model the stable and dynamic spatial relationships. One module employs a cosine-based attention mechanism with the explicit embedding of positional information about sites to capture stable inter-site relationships. Based on the air pollutant transport or dispersion conditions and the spatial information about the surrounding sites relative to the target site, the other module, derived from the Gaussian dispersion model, utilizes another attention mechanism to characterize dynamic relationships. Using records of air pollution and relevant factors from 253 sites around Xi'an, P.R. China, an empirical study verifies the effectiveness of the proposed model. Compared with other 10 reference models including some recently-developed spatiotemporal models, our proposed model significantly improves the one-day-ahead prediction of the concentrations of six pollutants. It further provides interpretability through the extracted attention weights to reveal the stable and dynamic relationships with a new perspective. The model offers statistical evidence for formulating policy with respect to air quality traceability and collaborative management.
