AB  - Electrostatically assisted coarse fiber filters hold great promise for overcoming the trade-off between high efficiency and low resistance in air filtration. However, the optimization of material synthesis and operational parameters is hindered by the complexity of multi-factor interactions and the high cost of exhaustive experiments. To address this challenge, we propose a machine learning-guided strategy. Using an artificial neural network (ANN) dual-objective prediction model, we accurately predicted filtration efficiency (n) and comprehensive quality factor (CQF), with superior performance compared to linear regression and random forest models. Model interpretability analysis revealed that discharge voltage is the dominant factor affecting n, while airflow velocity primarily determines the CQF. Notably, except for airflow velocity, all other features exert positive effect on both n and CQF. Partial dependence plots and interaction analyses identified optimal parameter ranges, showing that n and CQF cannot be maximized simultaneously but can achieve a favorable balance under specific conditions. Guided by these ranges, optimal synthesis and operation parameters were determined (in-situ growth time = 7 h, airflow velocity = 0.4 m/s, discharge voltage = 12 kV and the polarization voltage = 19 kV), yielding excellent filtration performance (n = 99.02 % and CQF = 0.451 Pa-1). Experimental validation confirmed the model predictions, demonstrating the reliability of the machine learning-guided optimization strategy. Overall, this study establishes a paradigm that integrates predictive modeling with experimental verification, providing a robust and efficient pathway for the rational design of next-generation electrostatically assisted fiber filters.
