AB  - Background: The interpretation of epidemiological evidence on ambient ultrafine particles (UFPs) is limited by uncertainties in exposures exposure due to unaccounted spatiotemporal variability. Objective: To develop models for estimating the spatiotemporal distribution of ambient UFPs in Shanghai, China. Methods: We established a study-specific monitoring network and repeatedly measured on-road UFP across three typical seasons. Using traffic, land use, air pollutants, and meteorological data, we constructed five prediction models based on both linear and nonlinear approaches, in single- and two-stage frameworks. Results: Mean (+/- standard deviation) of on-road UFP concentrations was 26,313 (+/- 8,932) N center dot cm- 3, showing pronounced spatial and temporal variations. The Random Forest (RF) one-stage model (R2 = 0.63, root mean squared error (RMSE) = 5463 N center dot cm- 3) and RF-based two-stage model (R2 = 0.62, RMSE = 5521 N center dot cm- 3) performed best, followed by Support Vector Regression (SVR) one-stage model (R2 = 0.57, RMSE = 5830 N center dot cm- 3), SVR-based two-stage model (R2 = 0.53, RMSE = 6146 N center dot cm- 3) and multiple linear regression model (R2 = 0.39, RMSE = 6975 N center dot cm- 3). External validation confirmed the superiority of the RF two-stage model. Conclusions: The models effectively captured spatiotemporal variation in UFP concentrations, supporting improved exposure assessment and health effect estimation.
