AB  - A nuanced understanding of the relationship between air quality and land use features is crucial for building sustainable and livable human settlements. We develop an urban land use measurement framework including the share of different urban land use types, land use size, mix, and intensity to evaluate their relationships with overall air quality and six major pollutants: PMS.5, PM10, SOS, NOx, CO, and O3. Using data from 265 Chinese cities between 2014 and 2020, we apply Gradient Boosting Decision Trees combined with Shapley Additive exPlanations to unravel their complex relationships in terms of non-linear relationships, threshold effects, and spatial heterogeneity. The results show that some land use features have linear relationships with air pollution, whereas others exhibit non-linear patterns. For example, although increasing green land can improve air quality, further expansion beyond a certain threshold results in little additional improvement. Moreover, better overall air quality is generally associated with residential land shares of 25-27 %, industrial land of 15-19 %, service land of 5-8 %, transport land of 20-25 %, and commercial land above 5 %, when other factors are constant. Finally, the impact of a given land use feature differs not only across pollutants but also across regions. For example, while land use mix is positively associated with most pollutants, its initial increase is associated with a reduction in NOx concentrations. Our findings contribute nuanced and policy-relevant insights that can support more effective land use planning for improving urban air quality.
