AB  - As China's air pollution control advances, ground-level ozone (O-3) has gradually replaced PM2.5 as the primary pollutant, threatening public health. Spanning COVID-19 control to economic recovery, 2019-2024 witnessed anthropogenic changes. Yet, understanding of O-3's spatiotemporal patterns, extreme-temperature sensitivity, and causal mechanisms during this period remains inadequate, limiting effective policy responses. To address these, this study, integrating ground-based O-3 observations, TROPOMI products, and ERA5 meteorological data, developed a BiTCN-Attention model to estimate China's 1km-resolution monthly average O-3 (2019-2024). 10-fold cross-validation confirmed its performance (R-2 = 0.91, RMSE = 6.96 mu g/m(3)), with stable spatial generalization (R-2 = 0.88) and monthly prediction ability (R-2 = 0.81-0.94). Spatiotemporal analysis revealed: (1) O-3 presented a "dual-core gradient expansion" pattern with high-concentration cores in the North China Plain (anthropogenically driven) and Northwest China (naturally driven); (2) nationwide O-3 increased significantly (0.94 mu g/m(3)/yr, p < 0.005) with high-concentration areas expanding inland; (3) during extreme summer heat (2022-2023), the intensity, position, and wind speed of the Western Pacific Subtropical High (WPSH) dictated O-3's regional dispersion patterns. Causal inference via double machine learning indicated temperature (ATE = 7.39) and solar radiation (ATE = 3.7) were core drivers with nonlinear thresholds (temperature > 17.16 degrees C, solar radiation > 157.94 W/m(2)). Regionally, Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei was temperature-dominated; Yangtze River Delta and Greater Bay Area showed diurnal "dilution-accumulation" by land-sea breeze; by topographic obstruction and stable weather, inland agglomerations (e.g., Chengdu-Chongqing) had rapid O-3/precursor accumulation. This study fills the gap in high-precision research on China's ground-level O-3 in 2019-2024.
