AB  - Climate change and urbanization are intensifying summer ozone (O3) episodes, which pose serious threats to public health, terrestrial ecosystems, and biodiversity. While exercise is essential for public health, exposure to elevated O3 concentrations undermines its benefits, especially in dense urban environments. This study combined wearable monitoring with machine learning to assess O3 exposure along jogging routes in Xi'an, China, and to derive generalizable insights for sustainable city planning. Results showed that evening O3 levels were on average 79.23 mu g/m3 higher than morning levels, with hotspots shifting from traffic corridors in the morning to open squares in the evening. Exposure drivers also varied diurnally: PM2.5 and temperature promoted O3 in the morning while tree canopy coverage reduced it (with diminishing returns beyond 40%), whereas relative humidity and urban canyon geometry dominated in the evening, alongside nonlinear vegetation effects. These findings reveal that the built environment strongly shapes O3 exposure risks and that safer activity times and spaces depend on local design. The implications extend beyond Xi'an: routing tracks away from traffic, enhancing multi-layered vegetation with adequate ventilation, and integrating humidity-sensitive designs can mitigate exposure globally. Embedding O3 management into urban health promotion and policy frameworks advances SDG 3 (Health and Well-being) and SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities), providing pathways to safeguard outdoor activity under climate change.
