AB  - Accurate and reliable air quality forecasting is crucial for precise decision-making in urban environmental management, building energy efficiency, and healthy ventilation systems. Due to variations in building functions and population distribution, air quality in different urban areas has significant differences in terms of health and economic impacts (referred to as "site importance"). However, traditional forecasting models often overlook this spatial heterogeneity, which limits their ability to support differentiated and refined environmental management strategies. To address this, this paper proposes a PM2.5 prediction model, which combines the whole sites' importance calculation and the spatiotemporal graph-convolutional-based fitting networks. First, we construct a multilayer air quality characterization network to illustrate the spatial and temporal dependencies between monitoring stations. Next, we use the fuzzy entropy method Fuzzy Tsallis eXtropy (FTE) to integrate the spatiotemporal relationships and influences within the multilayer air quality network and extract the importance of each station. Finally, the importance features of each station at each time are utilized in the FTE learning module, combined with the Graph Convolutional Network and Gated Recurrent Network module (GCNGRU), for PM2.5 prediction. Employing real-world historical data on air pollution and meteorology from the Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei region, we conducted comprehensive comparative and ablation studies. The experimental results indicate that the incorporation of station importance enables our model to surpass the best baseline, with average performance gains of 4.49 % in MAE, 6.39 % in RMSE, and 4.34 % in R 2 . This outcome validates the model's excellent capabilities for both short-term and long-term predictions across multiple stations.
