AB  - Given the persistent threat of severe fine particulate matter (PM2.5) pollution during the heating season in China's inland industrial cities to both public health and the environment, there is an urgent need to develop more targeted control strategies. An integrated framework was employed combining positive matrix factorization, the Hybrid Single-Particle Lagrangian Integrated Trajectory model, machine learning, and Shapley Additive exPlanation (PMF-HYSPLIT-ML-SHAP) to analyze the chemical composition, local sources, regional transport, and key drivers of PM2.5 in Linyi City across three consecutive heating seasons (Periods I, II, and III) from 2021 to 2024. Results indicated that organic matter and secondary inorganic ions collectively accounted for over 60 % of PM2.5 mass. Secondary nitrate formation process (SN) and secondary sulfates & coal burning (SS&CB) were identified as major sources. In contrast, firework combustion & industrial sources (FC&IS), which were absent in Period I due to a regulatory ban, emerged in subsequent periods. The RF model, optimized via RandomizedSearchCV and KFold cross-validation, achieved high predictive accuracy for PM2.5 concentrations. Driver analysis revealed that local emission sources constituted the most significant contribution to PM2.5 pollution in Linyi, with SN consistently identified as the dominant local factor across all heating seasons. Regional transport exhibited substantial temporal variation, with its contribution increasing from 12.6 % in Period I to 42.2 % in Period III, with the southwestern transport pathway representing the predominant external source. Key meteorological factors included ultraviolet radiation, relative humidity, and wind speed. These findings offer a scientific basis for formulating dynamic and region-specific pollution control strategies.
