AB  - The growing shift toward sustainable energy sources has positioned biodiesel-diesel blends as a promising renewable supplement for petroleum-based diesel. Despite its environmental benefits, its large-scale application faces obstacles such as reduced energy density, suboptimal cold-flow characteristics, elevated nitrogen oxide (NOx) emissions, and inconsistent engine operation. This review explores the strategic use of energetic additives, including alcohols, cetane improvers, nanoparticles, hydrogen, and water emulsions, to mitigate these challenges. Each category is analyzed based on its effects on engine performance indicators like brake thermal efficiency, combustion stability, and fuel utilization, as well as environmental impacts such as emissions of NOx, carbon monoxide, unburned hydrocarbons, and particulate matter. Noteworthy advancements, including the use of long-chain alcohols, AI-guided additive formulation, and nanomaterial-supported emulsions, are discussed for their ability to enhance combustion and emission characteristics. The review further examines innovations such as onboard hydrogen generation and advanced electrostatic filtration for ultrafine particle control. Practical considerations for real-time deployment, such as fuel compatibility, consistency in fuel quality, feedstock infrastructure, and adherence to regulatory standards, are also addressed. By integrating insights from various additive approaches and identifying key implementation barriers, this review provides a comprehensive outlook for the evolution of biodiesel technologies aligned with sustainability targets and circular economy principles.
