AB  - In this study, a Rapid Multi-Temporal Pollution Assessment System (RMT-PAS) has been developed based on a trend-decomposition explainable machine learning (ML) method, which was used to isolate and quantify the contributions of meteorology (MET) and anthropogenic emissions (EMIS) to the ambient PM2.5/O3 levels at different scales such as inter-annual, seasonal and typical pollution events. The RMT-PAS employed a stacking ensemble technique integrating the XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost models to establish the nonlinear response relationship between PM2.5/O3 pollution levels and multiple meteorological elements as well as emission components, and achieved high predictive accuracy. Inter-annual analysis (2015-2020) showed that reductions in EMIS accounted for 53.1 %-158.9 % of the observed decreases in annual-mean PM2.5, whereas for O3 the MET contribution increased substantially and displayed strong regional heterogeneity (-126.5 % to +48.6 %). Seasonal analyses indicated that MET contributions to PM2.5 exhibited the greatest fluctuation in winter, followed by autumn, spring, and summer (mean standard deviations: 5.13, 3.82, 2.95, and 2.00 mu g/m3). In contrast, for O3, the highest fluctuation occurred in summer, followed by autumn, spring, and winter (5.72, 4.70, 3.48, and 2.11 mu g/m3). The analysis of typical pollution events further revealed that PM2.5 pollution were mainly driven by boundary layer height (PBLH), relative humidity (RHU) and sea-level pressure (PRS_Sea), whereas O3 pollution were primarily controlled by temperature (TEM) and RHU. The developed RMT-PAS system can support government and environmental regulators in conducting long-term assessments and implementing timely, effective control measures during emergency events.
