AB  - Atmospheric polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) pose serious environmental and health risks due to their toxicity, persistence, and long-range transport potential. Yet, their seasonal dynamics, regional sources, and nonlinear regulatory mechanisms remain unclear in subtropical coastal regions influenced by monsoonal circulation. We monitored 23 parent and 8 derivative PAHs year-round in Xiamen, China, and applied PMF, trajectory analysis, and XGBoost to investigate their sources and nonlinear environmental drivers. The results revealed clear seasonal variation: particulate-phase PAHs peaked in winter (11.37 ng/m3) and declined in summer (0.13 ng/m3), while gaseous compounds showed the opposite trend. The gas-particle partition coefficients (log Kp) showed a strong positive correlation with the octanol-air partition coefficients (log Koa), while displaying a marked negative correlation with ambient temperature, reflecting the dual influence of compound traits and environmental conditions on phase behavior. PMF identified six major source factors, among which coal combustion and gasoline vehicle emissions were the leading contributors to wintertime toxicity, accounting for 56.73 % of the toxic equivalent (& sum;TEQ). Potential Source Contribution Function (PSCF) and Source Region Attribution (SRA) showed that the PAH pollution in Xiamen mainly comes from local source (34 %), northern and northeastern China (46 %) and Southeast Asia and south China Sea (20 %). SHAP-based interpretation of the XGBoost model demonstrated that low-ring PAHs were more sensitive to local meteorological conditions, while high-ring PAHs exhibited stronger association with air mass origins and particle-phase accumulation. These findings reveal seasonal PAH patterns in coastal air and underscore the importance of distinguishing local from transboundary sources.
