AB  - The effect of dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane and its metabolites (DDTs) on dyslipidemia remains unclear. The repeated-measures study was developed with 6389 observations from the baseline and 6-year follow-up of the Wuhan-Zhuhai cohort to assess associations of DDTs and genetic risks with dyslipidemia. Serum concentrations of o,p'-DDT, p,p'-DDT, o,p'-DDE, p,p'-DDE, o,p'-DDD, and p,p'-DDD, and serum levels of total cholesterol (TC), triglycerides (TG), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) were measured, dyslipidemia was diagnosed, and blood lipids-related polygenic risk scores (PRS) were constructed. The median levels of serum DDTs at baseline and during the 6-year follow-up were 18.3-3390.2 ng/L and 9.9-1707.7 ng/L, respectively. Cross-sectionally, associations were detected between DDTs and elevated TC, TG, and LDL-C, decreased HDL-C, and increased prevalent dyslipidemia risks. Longitudinally, each 1-unit increase in log-transformed values of p,p'-DDT was associated with the annual rate of change in TC of 0.014 (0.003 to 0.026) mmol/L, in TG of 0.015 (0.004 to 0.027) mmol/L, in LDL-C of 0.049 (0.038 to 0.060) mmol/L, in HDLC of -0.004 (-0.008 to -0.001) mmol/L, and a 32.7 % (4.1 % to 69.0 %) increase in incident dyslipidemia risks. The larger annual rates of change in lipids associated with p,p'-DDT were detected among females and the youngers than males and the elders. Additive interactions were found between DDTs and TG-related PRS on TG, the high PRS and high DDTs group exhibited more changes in TG compared with the low PRS and low DDTs group. PM2.5 showed the highest importance for serum total DDT levels in machine learning models, while repeated-measures analyses indicated that each 2-fold increase in PM2.5 was associated with a 1.537 % annual rate of increase in serum total DDT levels. In conclusion, DDTs and the interaction with PRS were associated with disrupted blood lipids and increased dyslipidemia risks, PM2.5 was the primary exposure route of DDTs.
