AB  - Solar radiation data are essential for designing and optimizing photovoltaic (PV) power systems. However, due to sparse and incomplete solar radiation measurements across many regions like Ethiopia, predictive modeling has become crucial for reliable solar resource assessment. The 12 machine learning (ML) and one Stacked models were trained by using ground-based observations from 2020 to 2022 at 16 Ethiopian synoptic weather stations. Meteorological data from the National Meteorological Institute of Ethiopia (NMI), alongside supplementary data including aerosol information, sky conditions, and other relevant parameters were also incorporated from the Modern-Era Retrospective Analysis for Research and Applications, Version 2, and National Aeronautics and Space Administration archives. The data from three Ethiopian weather stations (Bahir Dar, Arba Minch, and Harar) were excluded to assess model scalability and transferability. The dataset was partitioned, with 75% used for training and 25% for testing. The performance of the trained models was evaluated using metrics like correlation coefficient (R-2), Nash-Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE), mean absolute error, mean squared error, root mean squared error, normalized root mean squared error, and mean absolute percentage error. The stacked ensemble model demonstrated superior performance, achieving the highest accuracy in predicting daily and monthly solar radiation. This Stacked model, which outperformed Extreme Gradient Boosting, demonstrated exceptional performance with R-2 exceeding 0.95, NSE exceeding 0.94, and low error values for both daily and monthly predictions. This study demonstrates the effectiveness of ML techniques for accurately predicting solar radiation in Ethiopia. These results can significantly aid in designing and optimizing PV systems by reliably assessing potential energy production under various conditions.
