TI  - A novel system for aerosol classification and particle size identification using LIBS-SLIPA fusion with sliding window approach
T2  - OPTICS AND LASER TECHNOLOGY
LA  - English
KW  - Laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy
KW  - Laser-induced plasma acoustic signals
KW  - Support vector machine
KW  - Atmospheric aerosols
AB  - Atmospheric aerosols vary significantly in type and size, influencing their environmental behavior and health impacts, necessitating advanced identification and classification methods. This study introduces a novel LIBS-SLIPA system integrating Laser-Induced Breakdown Spectroscopy (LIBS) and Laser-Induced Plasma Acoustic (LIPA) signals for detecting aerosol types and sizes. A sliding window feature extraction algorithm, termed SLIPA, is developed to process LIPA signals, enhancing classification accuracy while reducing data dimensionality. The performance of LIBS, SLIPA, and the combined LIBS-SLIPA systems is evaluated using five machine learning models, with the Support Vector Machine (SVM) selected for classification. Hyperparameters of the SVM model are optimized via grid search to improve system performance. The recognition accuracy for oxides reached 98.5%, while the recognition accuracy for particle sizes reached 92.0%. The results indicate that the LIBS-SLIPA system demonstrates superior performance compared to LIPA in identifying aerosol types and particle sizes. The system successfully classified four oxide aerosol types and various silica aerosol particle sizes, demonstrating high stability and accuracy. These findings provide robust theoretical support for applying multi-source information fusion in atmospheric aerosol monitoring.
