AB  - Rapid economic development and intensified urbanization have led to a significant rise in PM2.5 pollution events, posing severe risks to public health and societal well-being. Accurate spatiotemporal forecasting of PM2.5 concentrations is crucial to mitigate these impacts. However, existing approaches that rely solely on numerical simulation or deep learning algorithms often suffer from limitations in forecasting accuracy, spatial resolution, and coverage. To address these challenges, this study presents a hybrid PM2.5forecasting framework that combines machine learning techniques with forecast data from GEOS-CF-a global atmospheric constituent prediction system that incorporates the GEOS-Chem chemistry module-alongside other high-resolution spatial and temporal predictors. The proposed framework provides 120-h, gap-free forecasts at a high spatial resolution of 1 km. The application of the Synthetic Minority Over-Sampling Technique (SMOTE) further improves the model's capability to forecast high PM2.5 concentrations by addressing data imbalances. The model was trained and validated under real-world conditions, ensuring that future data were excluded from the training process to prevent artificially inflated performance. Using China as a case study, validation results showed an average correlation coefficient (R) and root-mean-square error (RMSE) of 0.67 and 36.64 mu g/m3, respectively, for the first 12 h, and 0.48 and 39.39 mu g/m3 for the full 120-h forecast. This represents a significant improvement over GEOS-CF forecasts, which had R (RMSE) values of 0.33 (124.68 mu g/m3) and 0.22 (95.21 mu g/m3) for the same periods. The proposed framework also significantly reduces computational costs compared to running numerical models at a 1-km scale over large areas, enabling near-real-time, high-resolution PM2.5 forecasting, even in resource-constrained environments.
