AB  - Heatwaves represent one of the most significant extreme weather events affecting the northern regions of China during the summer. However, there is a relative paucity of knowledge concerning the planetary boundary layer height (PBLH) under heatwaves. This study conducts a systematic comparison of thermodynamic planetary boundary layer height (Ht) with material planetary boundary layer height (Hc) during heatwave events in Beijing. Generally, the PBLHs, whether thermodynamic or material, tends to be higher during heatwave periods as compared to non-heatwave periods, with the most noticeable differences occurring when the daytime mixed layer fully develops. In addition, Hc of 792 m is overall greater than Ht of 650 m during the Heatwaves. Influenced by a variety of meteorological and environmental factors, significant variations are observed in the heights of Ht and Hc during heatwaves. The biggest differences are typically observed in scenarios characterized by higher temperatures beyond 35 degrees C, lower humidity below 20 %, stronger southerly winds of 4-6 m s-1, and elevated O3 concentrations higher than 200 mg m-3. Moreover, with the well-developed convection of higher relative humidity (60-80 %) and weaker northwesterly winds (below 2 m s-1), Ht notably surpasses the Hc. In contrast, under the influence of lower relative humidity (below 20 %) and stronger southwesterly winds (4-6 m s-1), Hc presents a remarkably higher value than Ht. Whether daytime or nighttime, with the development of the planetary boundary layer and an increase (or decrease) in PM2.5 (O3) concentrations, Hc becomes greater than Ht, and the difference narrows down gradually. The variations in boundary layer height under heatwave predominantly depend on the differences in the characteristics of the boundary layer structures and the selected strategies for PBLH inversion. In the absence of a distinct temperature inversion layer at night, employing the top of the stable boundary layer as the height of the nocturnal stable boundary layer can result in an overestimation of Ht. Conversely, during the initial development of the daytime mixed layer and in the presence of nocturnal temperature inversions, Hc may be overestimated due to interference from other layers. (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
