AB  - This study examines the seasonal and spatial variations of naphthalene concentrations in central western Taiwan from 2020 to 2023, alongside the influence of meteorological conditions and emission sources. Data on naphthalene levels, related air pollutants (CO, NO2, NO, SO2, PM10, and PM2.5), and meteorological conditions were collected from ten monitoring stations. Pearson's correlation analysis revealed strong positive relationships between naphthalene and CO, NO2, PM10, and PM2.5, indicating common sources related to general combustion, such as biomass combustion, vehicular emissions, and industrial activities. Naphthalene levels peaked in autumn (62.8 ng/m(3)) and winter (60.6 ng/m(3)), driven by low temperatures, weaker photochemical reactions, and stagnant atmospheric conditions. Analysis of variance (ANOVA) revealed statistically significant seasonal differences (p < 0.01), with inland sites exhibiting greater accumulation under northeast monsoon conditions and local circulation patterns. To further understand the driving mechanisms, a machine learning model with SHAP analysis identified NO2 (20 %) and wind speed (19 %) as the most influential factors. Combustion-related pollutants accounted for approximately 33 % of naphthalene variation. Meteorological factors contributed 32 %, with wind speed being the dominant driver. These findings underscore the significant influence of meteorological conditions and emission sources on naphthalene distribution. The study highlights the importance of implementing integrated and season-specific air quality management strategies that target both gaseous and particulate pollutants to mitigate air pollution and protect public health in the region.
