AB  - The growing demand for sustainable energy and the limitations of fossil fuels have accelerated interest in advanced modeling approaches for biodiesel-fueled engines. This study presents a hybrid evaluation framework that integrates ensemble and boosting-based machine learning (ML) models with statistical design, sensitivity analysis, and SHAP-based interpretability to predict performance and emissions of a diesel engine operating with microalgae biodiesel blends. A pre-existing dataset comprising 64 observations across varying engine loads (25-100%), compression ratios (16.5-18.0), and blend levels (B20-B100) was analyzed. Six ML regression models, Decision Tree (DT), Random Forest (RF), Gradient Boosting (GB), Extra Trees (ET), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGB), and LightGBM, were trained to forecast five critical outputs: brake thermal efficiency (BTE), brake specific fuel consumption (BSFC), carbon dioxide (CO2), particulate matter (PM), and nitrogen oxides (NOx). Among these, GB demonstrated the best predictive performance with an average R2 of 0.999 (train) and 0.9586 (test) and MAPE of 2.58%, outperforming RF and XGB. Sensitivity analysis identified engine load as the most influential factor, while SHAP analysis further confirmed load and blend ratio as dominant predictors. Model robustness was validated via bootstrapping under L101 orthogonal design conditions, achieving low prediction uncertainty. Beyond predictive accuracy, the framework offers significant cost and time savings by reducing the need for extensive experimental trials. This study demonstrates a novel and practical approach for data-driven optimization of biodiesel-fueled engines, with implications for accelerating sustainable engine development.
