AB  - Urban air pollution is a critical environmental challenge that poses significant risks to human health, particularly for pedestrians on sidewalks due to their proximity to pollutants and lack of physical protection. To protect pedestrians' health, it is essential to monitor air pollution on sidewalk and identify the key contributing factors. While numerous fixed-site and vehicle-based mobile monitoring studies exist, regional-scale mobile air pollution monitoring specifically targeting sidewalks remains relatively underexplored. Among the various factors influencing urban air pollution, urban morphology plays a pivotal role. This study investigates pedestrian-level air pollution and its relationship with urban morphological indicators using mobile monitoring and machine learning techniques. Low-cost sensors were deployed to monitor PM2.5 concentrations along walkable roads in the Central Business District (CBD) of Shenzhen, China. To quantify the impact of urban morphology on pedestrian-level air pollution, a comprehensive integration of two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) urban morphological indicators was conducted, including 3D tree metrics and view indices extracted from street view images using advanced computer vision techniques. The results revealed significant spatiotemporal heterogeneity in the distribution of PM2.5 levels on sidewalk. By leveraging urban morphological indicators and machine learning models, the study achieved strong predictive performance (R2: 64.81 %-85.88 %). Variable interpretation analysis identified key factors, including building height, building volume, and the number of catering facilities, while also uncovering complex nonlinear relationships between 3D building and vegetation morphological indicators and air pollution. These findings provide actionable urban design and management strategies to mitigate pedestrian-level air pollution on sidewalks and promote healthier urban environments.
