AB  - On March 28, 2025, at 06:20:52 (UTC = LT-6.5), a powerful earthquake (Mw = 7.7) struck Myanmar (22.001 degrees N, 95.925 degrees E, depth similar to 10 km). In this study, the behavior of time series variations of 49 parameters obtained from 13 independent precursors including Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD), surface temperature, OLR (Outgoing Longwave Radiation), CO (Carbon Monoxide), NO2, Ozone, GPS-TEC (Total Electron Content), Ne (Electron Density), Te (Electron Temperature), Magnetic scalar and vectors quantities (B, Bx, By, Bz) obtained from satellite data in the period from January 1 to April 8, 2025, were analyzed in order to detect potentially seismic LAI (Lithospheric-Atmospheric-Ionospheric) anomalies. A large number of clear anomalies were observed in the 16-day period before the earthquake, and the possible LAIC (LAI Coupling) mechanism was discussed. Although ionospheric precursors and solar-geomagnetic parameters should be investigated more carefully due to the proximity of the earthquake epicenter to the equator and the effects of equatorial anomalies, multi-precursor analysis and the use of lithospheric and atmospheric precursors indicate prominent anomalies in the days prior to the earthquake. The implemented Fuzzy Inference System (FIS) predicts an earthquake with about magnitude of Mw = 7.7, 4 and 16 days before the main shock by fusion the number and also deviation of the observed anomalies from the defined permissible bounds. Therefore, the results of this study accompanied with confutation analysis emphasize the importance of studying earthquake precursors in different layers of the lithosphere, atmosphere, and ionosphere, conducting multi-precursor and multi-detector analysis, and utilizing precursor fusion systems to estimate earthquake parameters, as well as discussion the LAIC mechanism, in order to reduce uncertainty for the creation of earthquake early warning systems in the future.<br /> (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
