AB  - As urban development intensifies, underground spaces are increasingly utilized to maximize land efficiency. However, these areas often experience poor air quality due to limited ventilation, with PM2.5 accumulation being a major concern. While much research has focused on surface air quality, studies on underground air pollution are scarce. This study addresses this gap by collecting one-year PM2.5 and meteorological data from three locations-ground level, sunken plazas, and rooftops-within the world's largest underground complex. We conducted a comprehensive spatiotemporal analysis of PM2.5 concentrations, incorporating explainable feature engineering to enhance model interpretability. Features highly correlated with PM2.5 were selected using mutual information, ridge regression was employed to address multicollinearity, and recursive feature elimination was applied to refine the model. We introduce a novel integration of Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs) with classical time-series models for PM2.5 prediction. Hybrid models, including BILSTM-KAN, GRU-KAN, LSTM-KAN, TransformerKAN, and TCN-KAN, were developed to enhance prediction accuracy. Results show that air quality variations in underground locations are influenced by specific meteorological conditions, emphasizing the need for predictive models tailored to these unique environments. The integration of KANs significantly improved predictive performance with fewer parameters, outperforming traditional MLPs models in handling nonlinear spatial and temporal complexities. Notably, the TCN-KAN model achieved the highest accuracy in terms of R2 and error metrics. We highlight the potential of KANs as a powerful tool for urban and building environment modeling and represents the pioneering exploration of KANs within the domain, providing a more efficient and accurate solution framework.
