AB  - This study addresses the need for accurate, zone-specific air quality prediction in end-of-life vehicle (ELV) recycling environments by developing and optimizing six high-performance deep learning (DL) models. Motivated by the environmental and health concerns arising from formal and informal ELV recycling activities, this research leverages the green zone as a benchmark to evaluate prediction performance in other zones. The methodology involves deploying an in-house-developed device to collect real-time air quality and weather data across green, formal, and informal ELV recycling zones. Using Bayesian optimization, each DL model is tailored and fine-tuned for optimal performance in its respective zone. For the green zone benchmark, the Vanilla LSTM model performs best, with consistently lower errors, higher accuracy, and minimum bias across both pollutants, PM2.5 (MAE: 0.518; MAPE: 0.039) and PM10 (MAE: 0.929; MAPE: 0.042). For the formal ELV recycling zone, Vanilla GRU shows the best performance for both PM2.5 (MAE: 0.780; MAPE: 0.036) and PM10 (MAE: 1.438; MAPE: 0.49) predictions, which makes it the optimal model for the formal ELV recycling zone. Like the formal ELV recycling zone, the Vanilla GRU demonstrates superior performance for PM2.5 (MAE: 0.398; MAPE: 0.045) and PM10 (MAE: 0.691; MAPE: 0.056) prediction, establishing it as the optimal model for the informal ELV recycling zone. This work is the first to develop, optimize, and benchmark DL models for air quality prediction in ELV recycling zones. It contributes a comparative framework for zone-based model evaluation and provides actionable insights to support sustainable air quality management and policy development in emerging urban-industrial environments.
