AB  - Cities are expanding horizontally and vertically, yet their environmental impacts remain insufficiently quantified and understood. This study quantifies horizontal urban sprawl and vertical growth using county-level built-up area data in China from 2000 to 2020, assessing the environmental impact across three dimensions: climate change (carbon emissions, CE), environmental pollution (PM2.5 concentrations, PM), and ecological equity (bluegreen space exposure, BGS). By proposing a hybrid methodological framework integrating generalized additive model (GAM) and interpretable machine learning (IML), we unveil a greater environmental burden of vertical urban growth over horizontal sprawl. The results yield a consistent increase in urban sprawl index (SI) and vertical growth index (GI) over time. GAM reveals that the effects of SI, GI, and their interaction (SI_GI) on CE, BGS, and PM exhibit complex nonlinear patterns. The results of the IML highlight GI as a dominant positive contributor to both CE and PM, while SI shows relatively weaker effects. SI_GI contributes significantly to PM and moderately to CE, but has an insignificant negative effect on BGS. Further, with GAM we identify critical tipping points for each variable that mark shifts in environmental impact. These findings provide new insights into the environmental trade-offs of multidimensional urban growth and support the development of spatially adaptive policies for sustainable urbanization.
