AB  - Aerosol optical depth (AOD), a crucial optical feature of aerosols, plays a key role in evaluating air pollution and climate change. However, cloud contamination and surface conditions substantially impact the accuracy and reliability of AOD retrievals from satellite data. In recent years, several AOD reconstruction models have been developed to address the issue of missing satellite remote sensing data and improve spatiotemporal coverage. Although previous reconstruction methods have shown some success, their results remain constrained by the sparsity of ground-based observation sites, systematic biases among multisource datasets, and the heterogeneity of data accuracy. Moreover, current studies have not sufficiently exploited the valuable information within satellite-derived daily AOD data, further limiting the improvement of large-scale AOD reconstruction accuracy and hindering the potential for operational applications. A multi-layer LightGBM (LGBM) model that integrates multi-scale AOD spatiotemporal information (MLL-MST) is proposed to address these issues. This model aims to reconstruct the missing values in the latest Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) of MultiAngle Implementation of Atmospheric Correction (MAIAC) AOD products for China in 2023 on a large scale. The MLL-MST model reconstructs daily high-resolution AOD products without relying on ground-based observations. By employing a multi-layer LGBM architecture, it effectively extracts the spatiotemporal features embedded in the MODIS MAIAC AOD product. The reconstruction results are then assessed with ground truth data from the AErosol RObotic NETwork (AERONET) and the original MAIAC AOD product. Results indicate a strong consistency between the MLL-MST MAIAC AOD and AERONET AOD measurements, with a correlation coefficient (R) of 0.88 and a root mean square error (RMSE) of 0.293. The reconstructed daily AOD coverage for 2023, which was originally around 40 %, was improved to 100 %, demonstrating excellent spatiotemporal continuity. Furthermore, the reconstructed MLL-MST MAIAC AOD exhibits small deviation from the original MAIAC AOD and observational data from regional stations, capturing large-scale spatial distribution features and local AOD levels. To further validating the reliability and stability of the proposed reconstruction approach, this study systematically analyzed the spatiotemporal distribution characteristics of the reconstructed AOD.
