AB  - Timely and effective assessment of environmental sustainability is crucial for building sustainable cities and communities. However, most research focuses on inter-annual assessments of environmental sustainability, neglecting its potential seasonal variability. This study examined the seasonal variations in environmental sustainability in the Yangtze River Delta (YRD) from 2000 to 2020, using a Comprehensive Evaluation Index that integrates land surface temperature (LST), vegetation cover (VC), and PM2.5. The relative contributions of these three indicators alongside additional ecological and socioeconomic factors were also investigated through decomposition analysis and the XGBoost machine learning model. We found that environmental sustainability exhibited significant seasonal variations. The areas of environmental improvement in summer were 1.5 times those in winter, and the summer environmental degradation was half of winter's. Differences in environmental sustainability existed among different land-use types. Forests and grasslands degradation proportions were below 30 % in all seasons, whereas those of built-up areas exceeded 40 %. The contribution of the three indices to environmental sustainability changed with seasons and scales. At the regional scale, PM2.5 contributed the most throughout seasons. At the pixel scale, the major contributor showed spatial heterogeneity, with LST and VC contributing more in the southern part in winter and in the northern part in spring, respectively. Furthermore, water-related factors had greater importance, with soil moisture reaching 32.60 % in winter and potential evapotranspiration peaking at approximately 30 % in spring, summer, and autumn. Our findings provide new insights into the seasonal variation of environmental sustainability, and future environmental management should focus on the major contributors to environmental sustainability in different seasons.
