AB  - Aerosols, which envelop the entire Earth, significantly influence climate, environmental quality, and human health. Aerosol mapping is essential for understanding their interactions with meteorological and epidemiological factors, especially in regions with sparse ground stations. However, existing aerosol products face limitations in spatial coverage and resolution. To bridge this gap, we present a novel two-stage hybrid algorithm to generate seamless 1-km aerosol optical depth (AOD) maps across China from 2014 to 2023. In the first stage, an ensemble machine learning model is used to calibrate Modern-Era Retrospective analysis for Research and Applications, Version 2 (MERRA-2) AOD using same-day Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) aerosol retrieval. The calibrated MERRA-2 AOD is then used to fill gaps in the MODIS-derived product (i.e., MCD19A2), producing spatially complete aerosol maps as supervision labels. Subsequently, the second stage employs the labels and an enhanced Downscale-UNet model incorporating Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) and Depthwise Separable Convolution (DSC) modules to enhance spatial feature extraction and computational efficiency, ultimately obtaining seamless and high-resolution results through a surface-to-surface learning paradigm. Validation results show that the proposed model outperforms the conventional UNet by approximately 5 %, with a high determination coefficient (R2) of 0.93 and low root-mean-square error (RMSE) of 0.06 on the testing dataset. Independent validation against ground-based observations confirms its satisfactory performance with improved accuracy (R2 = 0.75) compared to MERRA-2 (R2 = 0.48) and superior spatial coverage (100 %) compared to MCD19A2 (44 %). Moreover, its spatial results demonstrate exceptional continuity and fine-scale feature representation, effectively bridging the complementary limitations of satellite retrievals and reanalysis data. This enables unprecedented aerosol analysis across both regional and climatological scales. Both the model and generated dataset are publicly accessible via https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15 847434, providing critical resources for air quality monitoring, epidemiological research, and climate modeling applications.
