AB  - Interpretable machine learning tools have enhanced the ability to model and analyze hourly variations in fine particulate matter (PM2.5) concentrations. These tools can distinguish the effects of multiple factors on PM2.5 levels and explore their non-linear relationships with PM2.5. This study analyzes hourly PM2.5 concentrations from two measurement sites in Kathmandu, Nepal, from 2017 to 2023. We use the Generalized Additive Model (GAM) to quantify the impacts of forest fires, long-range transport, local emissions, and meteorology on PM2.5 levels. GAM simulation shows that the seasonal temperature component is the dominant driver, explaining 36% of the model variance and indicating strong seasonal influences on PM2.5 concentrations. We use hourly back trajectories, combined with the Fire INventory from NCAR (FINN), to estimate the impact of biomass burning, which contributes approximately 8 mu g/m3 of the average 50 mu g/m3 PM2.5 at the Phora Durbar site, mainly from Nepal's forested areas. We determine the impact of long-range transport using Trajectory Cluster Concentration Impacts (TCCI) and observe that northwesterly air masses increase PM2.5 concentrations by up to 4 mu g/m3 while southeasterly air masses reduce concentrations by up to 4 mu g/m3. Diurnal variations and boundary layer height significantly influence PM2.5 levels, explaining 7% and 6% of the model variance, respectively, representing the influence of local emission. Overall, the GAM framework effectively quantifies the contributions of meteorology, forest fires, long-range transport, and local emissions to PM2.5 concentrations, highlighting the necessity of addressing these factors to improve air quality.
