AB  - Existing ecological quality assessment model, such as Remote Sensing-based Ecological Index (RSEI), primarily rely on vegetation, humidity, land surface temperature and dryness indicators. The integration of air quality indicators, such as Particulate Matter (PM2.5), into RSEI remains limited, which also factor that play a crucial role in ecosystem health. This study aims to explore the ecological quality for Northeast Sichuan from 2003 to 2023 using a modified RSEI (MRSEI) by adding Particulate Matter 2.5 (PM 2.5) as well as replaced NDVI with KNDVI using Google Earth Engine. The ecological quality trends are further evaluated using the principal component analysis method, Theil-Sen + Mann-Kendall test, and Geodetector. The results indicate that: (1) MRSEI outperformed RESI in assessing ecological quality of Northeast Sichuan based on the Pearson correlation and image comparison analysis; (2) The MRSEI model demonstrates strong explanatory power, with the average variance explained by the first principal component exceeding 76 %. From 2003 to 2023, Northeast Sichuan experienced significant ecological improvement, with the mean MRSEI increasing by 24.0 % from 0.43 to 0.54. The results reveal a spatial pattern of "superior conditions in the north and stability in the south"; (3) The Theil-Sen-MK analysis indicates that 88.45 % of the region has experienced positive ecological trends, exhibiting an "east-superior-to-west and south-superior-to-north" spatial differentiation pattern, though some ecological degradation persists in the mountainous areas in the western part of Guangyuan; and (4) Geodetector analysis reveals the dominant influence of the thermal environment and air quality on ecological evolution, while also revealing an increasing impact of anthropogenic activities. These findings provide valuable theoretical insights and practical guidance for ecological conservation and sustainable development initiatives in West China. (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
