AB  - This article presents a model predictive control (MPC) of energy recovery ventilators (ERVs) for maintaining required concentration levels of PM2.5, PM10, and CO2 in a real-life preschool classroom, while minimizing energy use of ERVs. For this purpose, the authors developed an artificial neural network (ANN) model that can predict time-varying concentrations of PM2.5, PM10, and CO2 (within normalized mean bias error: 0.8%, coefficient of variance root mean square error: 13.2%). In addition, the authors developed a stochastic occupancy model to predict occupant presence in the classroom. As a result, the real-time MPC could maintain required indoor concentration levels of PM2.5, PM 10, and CO2 in the classroom during the weekdays (baseline: 83.1% (four days), MPC: 96.1% (four days)) and achieved an energy-saving rate of 6.5%. This study suggests that the ANN MPC can accurately predict the time-varying indoor environment in the classroom as well as be beneficially used for energy savings.
