AB  - With the rapid expansion of urban subway systems, air quality in enclosed underground environments has become a public health concern. Despite widespread use of Platform Screen Door (PSD) systems, limited attention has been paid to how particulate matter (PM) behaves under varying operational conditions. This study investigates the spatiotemporal distribution and diffusion mechanisms of PM2.5 and PM10 across 60 stations on five metro lines in Hefei, China, using synchronized mobile monitoring and a combined Self-Organizing Map (SOM) and Generalized Estimating Equation (GEE) framework. Results show that PM2.5 concentrations are highest at entrances, while PM10 peaks on platforms-exceeding outdoor levels by up to 23.44 mu g/m(3). Correlation between outdoor PM2.5 (within 800-1800 m) and indoor levels varied by line, reaching r = 0.71 in some cases. GEE modeling revealed stratified diffusion patterns: at low-flow stations, PM2.5 was strongly influenced by ambient pollution, and PM10 was dominated by train-induced mechanical turbulence (beta = 6586.9, p < 0.001). At medium-flow stations, temperature (beta = -2.84, p = 0.005) and humidity (beta = -0.69, p = 0.012) differences helped mitigate PM10. In high-flow stations, PM10 concentrations were primarily driven by internal resuspension, while PM2.5 remained tied to external sources. These findings highlight distinct diffusion mechanisms by passenger flow strata and support the use of SOM-GEE for quantifying multi-source PM dynamics in complex subway environments.
