AB  - Background: Ambient fine particulate matter (PM2.5) is a significant global public health concern. However, largescale research has yet to explore the effects of long-term PM2.5 exposure on the pulmonary nodule characteristics. Methods: A lung cancer screening project involving 19,414 adults who underwent chest CT scans was conducted as part of the Integrating Artificial Intelligence and Serum Protein Biomarkers cohort between 2019 and 2020. Seven lag exposure variables were created to investigate PM2.5 effects, including five chemical constituents (organic matter, black carbon, sulfate, nitrate, ammonium) on the occurrence of positive pulmonary nodules and dominant nodule features. Two-pollutants models were fitted to examine the independent effect of PM2.5. Subgroup analyses considered demographic, behavioral, health, and meteorological factors. Results: Annual residential PM2.5 levels ranged from 17 mu g/m3 to 61 mu g/m3 (average: 23 mu g/m3). We found that exposure to PM2.5 with a 3-year lag, particularly with elevated sulfate and black carbon levels, was associated with an increased rate of positive nodules and a greater number of such nodules. Besides, the dominant nodules were more likely to be larger in volume, be located in the lower lung lobes and be solid. The effect of PM2.5 was more prominent in specific subgroups such as those with a history of lung disease and exposed to high temperature. Conclusions: This large-scale study provides first-hand epidemiological evidence that long-term exposure to ambient PM2.5 and certain constituents, particularly sulfate and black carbon, are associated with increased pulmonary nodule risk, elucidating the early effects of environmental factors on lung health.
