AB  - Surface ozone (O3) is a critical air pollutant that has adverse effects on human health, environment, and air quality. This study introduces a hybrid approach combining principal component (PC) analysis and stepwise multiple linear regression into PC regression (PCR) and PC - artificial neural network (PC-ANN) models. It aims to address multicollinearity in O3 prediction, improve model performance, and identify key factors influencing surface O-3 levels in Mosul, Baghdad, and Basrah using data from 2020 to 2022. The analysis included surface O3, air pollutants (NO2, CO, SO2, PM2.5, and PM10), and meteorological parameters, including temperature, total precipitation, boundary layer height, surface pressure, wind speed, and wind direction. These variables were examined to evaluate their effects on the surface O3 concentrations. Varimax rotation identified three PCs for Mosul and Baghdad, explaining 68.107% and 71.827% of the variance, respectively, and four PCs for Basrah, accounting for 73.372% variance. Both the PCR and PC-ANN addressed multicollinearity and performed well; however, the PC-ANN outperformed the PCR in all three provinces. The R2 values for PCR were 0.732 (Mosul), 0.848 (Baghdad), and 0.760 (Basrah), whereas the PC-ANN yielded higher values of 0.8310, 0.8860, and 0.9044, respectively, indicating better accuracy and lower error.
