AB  - Understanding particulate matter (PM) accumulation, dispersion, and transport within and above surface mines is crucial for protecting the health of mine workers and nearby communities. However, such assessments are often limited by measurement challenges. This study presents an advanced method that combines low-cost PM sensors with an unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) to monitor real-time airborne PM of different sizes within and above a 130 m deep large-scale surface chromite mine. Results showed higher PM concentrations near active mining operations, followed by a gradual decline within 8-20 m of vertical height. Mean PM mass (PM1, PM2.5, PM10) concentrations recorded in-pit (54, 67, 128 mu g m-3) were significantly higher than the out-pit values (48, 57, 83 mu g m-3), indicating 11-35 % reduction in the vertical direction. Finer particles were more dominant at higher altitudes with UAV-to-source ratios of 0.9 (PM1), 0.8 (PM2.5), and 0.5 (PM10). A decision tree-based random forest model trained using realistic and site-specific input features performed with reasonable accuracy (R2 >= 0.85; RMSE <= 0.11) in predicting PM at higher altitudes. Vertical depth of the mine was found to be the most important parameter (40 %) in explaining the variability of PM concentration in the vertical direction. The outcomes of this study provide detailed measurements of size-resolved PM mass concentrations associated with major mining activities across the vertical depth of the surface mine. The findings serve as a critical basis for evaluating the exposure risks faced by different mine worker groups and adjoining communities.
