AB  - The growing need for sustainable and clean energy solutions has led to significant research on alternative fuels for internal combustion engines. This study investigates the performance and emission characteristics of a dualfuel (DF) compression ignition engine fueled with a renewable blend of neem oil methyl ester (NeOME B20), producer gas (PG), and hydrogen (H2). NeOME B20 was used as a pilot fuel while PG and H2 served as primary gaseous fuels. Experimental trials were conducted using a six-hole injector (0.2 mm) and an optimized injection timing of 27 degrees bTDC. Among the tested configurations, the optimal fuel flow rates-0.000151 kg/s (NeOME B20), 0.005 kg/s (PG), and 0.00000122 kg/s (H2) resulted in a maximum brake thermal efficiency (BTE) of 22.18 %, with notable reductions in emissions: hydrocarbons (HC) decreased to 39.94 ppm, carbon monoxide (CO) to 0.28 %, and particulate matter (PM) was significantly reduced. However, nitrogen oxides (NOx) increased to 204.89 ppm (0.0205 %), due to improved combustion and higher in-cylinder temperatures. The ignition delay and combustion duration were also reduced under these optimized conditions. To enhance predictive capabilities and reduce experimental effort, multiple machine learning (ML) models including Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and SVM were employed. Among these, the Gradient Boosting model demonstrated superior performance with an R2 of 0.999 and the lowest RMSE, confirming its effectiveness for predicting engine performance and emissions. These findings highlight the potential of integrating renewable fuels with ML-based optimization to develop sustainable and high-efficiency dual-fuel engine technologies.
