AB  - This study presents an innovative approach to mitigate the emissions by substituting conventional diesel fuel with biodiesel blends derived from Momordica charantia, augmented with TiO2 nanoparticles. We investigated four distinct fuel formulations: D90BG10+25ppm TiO2, D80BG20+50ppm TiO2, D70BG30+75ppm TiO2, and BG100+100 ppm TiO2. Each blend was subjected to a series of three trials conducted at 1500 rpm, with comprehensive uncertainty analysis to ensure robustness of results. Additionally, three advanced machine learning algorithms were employed to forecast the performance and emission behaviours of the engine. This study reveals that the incorporation of TiO2 nanoparticles into Momordica charantia biodiesel blends yields substantial enhancements in engine performance and emission reductions. Specifically, the D90BG10+25 ppm TiO2 blend exhibited 7.25% improvement in brake thermal efficiency and a 31.88% reduction in brake-specific fuel consumption relative to conventional diesel. Furthermore, the particulate matter emissions associated with these blends were markedly lower. Notably, the BG100+100 ppm TiO2 blend resulted in 0.18 g/kWh of carbon monoxide and 0.65 g/kWh of hydrocarbon emissions. In contrast, the D90BG10+25ppm TiO2 blend demonstrated the lowest carbon dioxide emissions, at 3 g/kWh, compared to 11 g/kWh for the BG100+100 ppm TiO2 blends. Statistical evaluation demonstrated that the Boost model outperformed other machine learning models.
