AB  - The technology for retrieving precipitable water vapor (PWV) using Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) has already reached maturity, and it shows a relatively strong correlation with PM2.5. Nevertheless, there is a lack of specifically developed algorithms for the relationship between PWV and PM2.5. This study focused on the southern region of China, data from GNSS stations, radiosonde (RS) stations, and co-located pollution monitoring stations in southern China during 2019-2022 were collected. The correlations between PM2.5 and meteorological data, such as PWV, water vapor pressure, pressure, and temperature, were thoroughly investigated. It was found that the correlations between PWV and PM2.5 were generally high yet exhibited regional variations. Based on these findings, a stacked machine learning (SML) model was innovatively developed for estimating PM2.5 concentrations. During the model construction process, random forest (RF) and back-propagation neural network (BPNN) sub-models were developed, and the SML model was constructed by integrating the advantages of the two through the multiple linear regression model. The SML model performed outstandingly, its R2reached 0.88, and the root mean square error (RMSE) and mean absolute error (MAE) were significantly reduced compared with those of other models. It demonstrated a high-precision estimation ability in medium and low concentration intervals (0-10, 10-20, 20-30 mu g/m3), with MAE and RMSE being respectively less than 4.50 mu g/m3and 5.90 mu g/m3. On an hourly scale, the SML model exhibited stable performance, with R2ranging from 0.84 to 0.93. In addition, the study also revealed that the PM2.5 concentrations in southern China had significant regional characteristics, with the highest levels in the northeastern region and the lowest in the western region, which were closely related to terrain and population factors. (c) 2025 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
