AB  - The concentration of water-soluble inorganic ions (WSIIs) in dust is comparatively higher than in soil, attributed to atmospheric deposition processes. Combining heavy metals (HMs) and WSIIs in dust for source analysis can more accurately identify and control the pollution sources of dust in megacity parks. Quantitative analysis and machine learning methods were used to investigate the source characteristics, health risks, and prioritized control elements of WSIIs (NO3-, SO42-, etc.) and HMs (As, Cd, etc.) in 144 surface dust samples from Chengdu, China. The results revealed that WSIIs and HMs were generally high where major traffic arteries intersected, with As, Cd, Ca2+ and SO42-being the main characteristic elements. Natural sources contributed to the total input of HMs has reached the highest (25.6 %), followed by mixed sources (19.6 %), indicating the effectiveness of emission reduction measures in recent years, while the effect of atmospheric deposition on surface dust composition may be intensifying. Combining the self-organizing map (SOM) with the absolute principal component scores-multiple linear regression (APCS-MLR), about 60 % of the points are classified into Clusters I and V, indicating that transportation and industrial sources are still dominant. The noncarcinogenic risk of HMs can be negligible. Nearly all populations faced an acceptable carcinogenic risk, primarily from exposure to As and Cr. Biomass and coal combustion were recognized as major sources of health risks. In conclusion, the effect of atmospheric deposition on surface dust in Chengdu is becoming more pronounced, necessitating a shift in the future focus of prevention measures.
