AB  - Industrial agglomeration areas have emerged as an important form of intensive production; however, the environmental pollution or odor complaint caused by volatile organic compounds has elicited widespread concerned due to the agglomeration of chemical companies. In order to trace source of disturbing odor pollutants or higher reactive pollutants for ozone formation, many industrial areas have conducted study and application of pollution tracing at different levels within and around the boundaries of enterprises and industrial areas, but many problems remain unaddressed. Given the demonstrated capability of artificial intelligence to classify problems, based on Bayesian optimization, a multimodel algorithm was developed to accurately identify and classify the sources of volatile organic compounds. Comparing the models of different algorithms, the convolutional neural network-long short-term memory model demonstrated the best predictive performance (accuracy of 0.886). Furthermore, comparison of two feature importance analysis tools (Out-Of-Bag and SHapley Additive exPlanations) indicated that benzene, toluene, and isobutane have the greatest impacts on tracing results, reflecting the interaction mechanisms among them. This paper demonstrates that the application of machine learning and neural networks can achieve efficient and accurate source identification of volatile organic compounds. The intelligent approach developed here serves as a successful precursor for establishing advanced identification and classification systems and holds broad application prospects in pollutant tracing for chemical industrial park to improve the scientific and accurate management.
