TI  - Multiple sites air quality prediction using a novel deep learning model integrating multi-scale spatiotemporal features and spatial heterogeneity
T2  - PROCESS SAFETY AND ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION
LA  - English
KW  - Air quality forecasting
KW  - PM 2.5 concentrations
KW  - Multi-scale spatiotemporal information
KW  - Spatial heterogeneity
KW  - POLLUTION
KW  - PARTICLE
KW  - PM2.5
AB  - Air pollution is a significant environmental issue that poses serious risks to both human health and ecosystems. Multi-site air quality predictions that incorporate spatiotemporal features can provide valuable insights for urban environmental governance and residents' daily health management. However, current AI-based models face two critical research gaps that may lead to poor forecasting performance. First, these models typically capture spatial features at only a single time scale, while spatial characteristics can vary across different time scales, such as daily or weekly. Second, many models rely on a single-structure Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network to extract temporal features, which overlooks the spatial heterogeneity of air pollution across various geographical locations. To address these gaps, this study proposes a novel multi-scale spatiotemporal convolution network (MS-STCN), which integrates the strengths of Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), Graph Convolutional Network (GCN), and Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN). Specifically, MS-STCN uses FFT to effectively identify the k most important time scales and aggregates information from multiple sites across these scales using GCN. Furthermore, the TCN employs multiple convolutional kernels to enhance the capture of spatial heterogeneity among different sites. The forecast results for PM2.5 concentrations using the MS-STCN model are 7.17 % in MAPE, 2.83 mu g/m3 in RMSE, 2.06 mu g/m3 in MAE, 8.31 mu g/m3 in MSE, and 0.94 in R2. Compared to other models, the MS-STCN demonstrated improvements ranging from 9.75 % to 44.27 %, 9.47-44.21 %, 8.80-43.47 %, 19.10-69.10 %, and 2.03-21.56 % for the respective evaluation indices. The DM test results show that these improvements are statistically significant at least at the 10 % significance level. These results highlight the superiority of the MS-STCN model in providing timely and precise multi-site air quality forecasts, as well as its substantial potential for aiding policymakers in adapting and refining pollution control strategies to better tackle air quality issues.
