AB  - The increasing adoption of electric vehicles has created an emerging mixed traffic scenario, altering the influence of traffic-related air pollution on public health. However, existing quality models rarely focus on multi-type vehicles. In this study, we developed an explainable machine-learning model to explore the complex relationship between multi-type vehicles and pollution. Road-level air quality data from taxi-based mobile monitoring system were calibrated through XGBoost model. And the SHAP explainer was employed to quantify the contribution divers to air pollution. Results showed the addition of categorized traffic notably enhanced model performance of PM2.5, PM10, NO2 by 24%, 27% and 59%, respectively. Light passenger vehicles contribute most to air pollution, but less than their traffic share. Diesel and China emission standard vehicles are major contributors, underscoring the need for targeted controls. And the vehicle electrification experiment demonstrates that our proposed model is practical green traffic management.
