AB  - The catalytic degradation of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) by transition metals, notably iron, on the surface of clay has been demonstrated in laboratory settings, yet field-based evidence in atmospheric particulate matter (PM) remained scarce due to confounding factors such as shared emission sources and intricate multipollutant interactions. This study employed explainable machine learning to quantitatively resolve relationships between PAH concentrations and metal content in PM, while probing mechanistic interactions. Models were trained using comprehensive field data collected in South Korea (2013-2017), with XGBoost demonstrating superior performance (R2 = 0.91). Mechanistic interpretation suggests potential catalytic degradation of PAHs by Fe and corresponding reduction in cancer and non-cancer risks, with single electron transfer (SET) and Fenton reactions as proposed mechanisms. We uncovered possible synergistic interactions between Fe and co-occurring metals (e.g., Mn and Ti) in catalyzing PAH degradation. This study provides the first field evidence of metalcatalyzed PAH degradation in ambient PM. Our findings underscore the pivotal role of metal-organic interactions in modulating PM-related health risks. This offers insights for optimizing emission controls and for guiding catalyst development for PAH removal. Further laboratory studies are necessary to validate the proposed mechanisms and identify optimal conditions.
