AB  - Cities are crucial in achieving global climate goals and advancing low-carbon development. As the world's largest developing economy and leading CO2 emitter, China faces the complex challenge of balancing economic growth with pollution control and carbon reduction. This study proposes a novel analytical framework that integrates the Non-Radial Directional Distance Function (NDDF) with the Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), complemented by Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP), to examine the coordinated abatement costs of PM2.5 and CO2 across 287 Chinese cities from 2006 to 2019. The NDDF enables a robust estimation of environmental efficiency and marginal abatement costs (MAC) for both pollutants. At the same time, the LightGBM-SHAP approach provides interpretable insights into the nonlinear and heterogeneous effects of socioeconomic and environmental drivers. Theoretically, this integration bridges the gap between parametric frontier analysis and data-driven machine learning, enhancing the accuracy and interpretability of environmental performance assessments. Empirically, the study reveals an inverted U-shaped temporal trend in joint marginal abatement costs (JMAC) and pronounced regional disparities. Abatement costs are highest in economically advanced urban clusters such as the Yangtze River Delta, Pearl River Delta, and Chengdu-Chongqing region. Labor force and financial activity are identified as key drivers of JMAC for PM2.5, whereas employment structure and industrial SO2 emissions are the dominant factors for JMAC related to CO2. Moreover, as city size decreases, the influence of energy consumption diminishes, while demographic and economic variables become more significant. Policy implications suggest the need for regionally differentiated emission reduction strategies, targeted investments in clean technologies and green finance, and policy support for the labor and financial sectors to reduce abatement costs and promote urban sustainability. These findings offer practical insights for formulating regionally differentiated and sector-specific policies, supporting more effective and coordinated emission reduction strategies across urban China.
