AB  - In the context of rapid urbanization, PM2.5 pollution poses a significant threat to public health and environmental quality. Current spatiotemporal analysis methods often lack sufficient accuracy. To address this, this study uses spatiotemporal analysis and Spatial Temporal Graph Convolutional Networks (ST-GCN) to evaluate the variation and driving factors of PM2.5 concentrations in the Beijing-Tianjin-Hebei (BTH) urban agglomeration from 2014 to 2024, and to make predictions. ST-GCN effectively captures spatial features and temporal dependencies of PM2.5, revealing intercity relationships more accurately. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) provide model interpretability, identifying key factors influencing PM2.5 concentrations. The results from experiments on the BTH dataset are as follows: (1) The proposed algorithm accurately captures the spatiotemporal characteristics of pollution in the BTH urban agglomeration, with an R-2 of 0.96. (2) PM2.5 concentrations in the BTH urban agglomeration decreased significantly from 2014 to 2024, with a cumulative reduction of 60.00%, especially in the southwestern areas, though annual averages still exceeded national standards. (3) SHAP analysis shows that PM10 and CO are the main positive contributors to PM2.5 concentrations. (4) From 2025 to 2030, PM2.5 concentrations are expected to decline, with a 2030 concentration of 34.47 mu g m(-3).
