TI  - Forecasting PM2.5 concentrations with spatial-temporal hypergraph attention network: Modeling geospatial correlations and latent associations among stations
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - PM2.5 forecast
KW  - Spectral clustering
KW  - Graph attention networks
KW  - Hypergraph attention networks
KW  - Gated recurrent units
KW  - PREDICTION
AB  - PM2.5 is a ubiquitous air pollutant that poses substantial health risks and can lead to respiratory diseases. Consequently, accurate forecasting of PM2.5 concentrations is of paramount importance for the effective management of urban air pollution. Traditional PM2.5 forecasting methods focus primarily on pairwise station correlations but fall short in capturing higher-order relationships, such as synergy, heterogeneity, and cascading interactions among multiple stations. This study introduces the Spatial-Temporal Hypergraph Attention Network (STHGAT), which aims to forecast PM2.5 concentrations by leveraging hypergraphs to model high-order relationships among monitoring stations, including geospatial correlations and latent associations. The STHGAT framework comprises three core modules: multi-graph construction, spatial feature extraction, and multi-view feature fusion. In the multi-graph construction module, graph is created using geographic information thresholds, geographical hypergraph is formed within specified threshold intervals, and clustering hypergraph is derived using spectral clustering algorithms. For spatial feature extraction, STHGAT employs the Hypergraph Attention Networks (HGAT) to capture high-dimensional spatial features and the Graph Attention Networks (GAT) to learn adjacency characteristics within graph. The multi-view feature fusion module combines features from both hypergraphs and graphs, leveraging Gated Recurrent Units (GRU) to capture temporal dynamics. The experimental results indicate that this method achieves a forecast accuracy of 78% on the Beijing air quality dataset, surpassing the second-best baseline model by 2.81%. In validations conducted across other regions, the forecast accuracy remains consistently around 77%, demonstrating substantial robustness. STHGAT employs hypergraphs to model multi-modal cascading relationships, thoroughly integrating the spatial-temporal propagation patterns of pollutants.
