AB  - American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius) is globally popular because of its medicinal and edible value. A geographical origin identification study is urgent to prevent economic adulteration. The aim of this study was to propose an integrated strategy via combining machine learning with fusion of multisource polysaccharide data based on hydrophilic interaction chromatography-charged aerosol detection for the geographical origin identification of American ginseng. Four machine learning algorithms (SVM, MLP, LDA and GBDT) combined with single or multisource polysaccharides (monosaccharide composition and polysaccharide depolymerized products profile) data were evaluated for the geographical origin authentication of American ginseng samples from different regions. As a result, most of the machine learning algorithms combined with low-level polysaccharide data fusion exhibited better classification efficiency than the single or middle-level fusion data. Among them, LDA can satisfactorily classify the American ginseng samples for both between the protected designation of origin (PDO) and non-PDO or among the non-PDO regions with 100 % accuracy for both the training and test sets. Thus, this study proved that the developed multisource of polysaccharide data fusion assisted by machine learning had great potential in the geographical origin authentication of American ginseng, which could also be used for the authenticity research of other foods rich in polysaccharides.
