AB  - Air pollution, particularly fine particulate matter (PM2.5), is a major global health threat, linked to respiratory, cardiovascular, and neurodegenerative diseases. To investigate the toxicological mechanisms of air pollutants and their health effects, we developed R-APEX, a knowledge graph (KG) platform integrating data from 24 biomedical databases and over 800 peer-reviewed publications. The complete KG contains 91,661 nodes and 10,550,809 edges, encompassing 11 entity categories and 75 relation types. R-APEX maps both curated and predicted associations, presented via interactive visualization. It leverages knowledge graph embedding (KEG) techniques to predict relationships between pollutants, genes, and diseases. These predictive capabilities support customized analyes for research needs, facilitating insight into how air pollutants contribute to human diseases, and aiding treatment discovery. Futhermore, R-APEX enables multidimensional analysis hypothesis generation, and the development of research plans. It servers as a valuable resource for academic researchers exploring complex links between air pollution and biological systems, which supporting the validation of predictive models. All data and the platform are freely available at https://rapex.prophetdb.org, with the source code hosted on GitHub at https://github.com/open-prophetdb/biomedgps-data.
