KW  - environmental monitoring
KW  - SUPPORT VECTOR MACHINE
KW  - REGRESSION
AB  - It is challenging to know which is the best technique for predicting PM2.5 concentration in open-pit copper mines. Because fine particulate matter is of great environmental and occupational safety significance in open-pit copper mines. Therefore, a modelling approach was performed by establishing a dynamic weighting and genetic algorithm-based hybrid SVR-Ridge model (DW-GA-SVRR) to evaluate the effectiveness of PM2.5 prediction in open-pit copper mines, and the Sin Quyen copper mine (Vietnam) was selected as a case study with a short-term pilot dataset collected from 09 April to 5 June 2024 with various timestamps. While this duration does not capture full seasonal variability, it allows for initial assessment of intra-daily and weekly dynamics relevant to mine operations. Feature engineering techniques, including lag-based variables and meteorological interaction features, were employed to capture temporal and nonlinear dependencies. Regularisation terms were introduced in the optimisation process to prevent overfitting and improve model robustness. Experimental results demonstrate that the DW-GA-SVRR model outperforms individual machine learning and statistical models in both training and testing phases, achieving high accuracy in capturing short-term PM2.5 fluctuations. The findings highlight the importance of hybrid modelling and optimisation strategies in environmental prediction tasks. This work contributes a scalable and practical framework for real-time air quality forecasting in open-pit mining, supporting sustainable operations and occupational health management.
