AB  - There has been a growing interest in revealing the impact of digital inclusive finance on air quality. However, most of the existing studies are limited by strong assumptions of traditional models regarding functional forms and lead to the relationship between digital inclusive finance and air quality underexplored. To address this issue, this study uses machine learning to investigate how digital inclusive finance affects air quality from a nonlinear perspective and detect the potential interactions. Based on city-level data from China, this study finds the impact of digital inclusive finance on air quality initially presents as an improvement effect and then shifts to a deteriorating effect. Energy consumption plays a key role in mediating this nonlinear effect. This study also detects that digital inclusive finance differently interacts with economic development, population density, and the level of industrial development. By accounting for both the nonlinear relationship and the interactions, the development of digital inclusive finance reduces PM2.5 by 16.4 mu g/m3 at the median level from 2011 to 2019, approximately equivalent to 40 % of the effect of China's clean air policies. By machine learning, this study enriches the understanding of how digital inclusive finance affects air quality from the perspective of nonlinear relationship and interactions and helps to evaluate the benefits of digital inclusive finance on air quality improvement more precisely. Each city can develop differentiated policies based on its development of digital inclusive finance and other socio-economic conditions to better achieve environmental sustainability goals.
